        "title": "Greenhouse gases",
        "text": "Greenhouse gases come from a wide range of sources, and their rising concentration is largely related to the combined effects of a growing population, higher living standards and changes in lifestyle. From a current base of 5 billion, the United Nations predicts that the world's population may stabilise in the twenty-first century at between 8 and 14 billion, with more than 90 per cent of the projected increase taking place in the developing nations. The activities needed to support that growth, particularly producing the energy and food required, will cause further increases in greenhouse gas emissions. The challenge, therefore, is to achieve a sustainable balance between population, economic growth and the environment.\n\nThe major greenhouse gases produced by human activities are carbon dioxide (CO2), methane and nitrous oxide. Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) are the only major contributor to the greenhouse effect that does not occur naturally; they come from sources such as refrigeration, plastics and manufacturing. Coal's total contribution to greenhouse gas emissions is thought to be about 18 per cent, with about half of this coming from electricity generation.\n\nThe coal industry worldwide devotes extensive resources to researching and developing new technologies and ways of capturing greenhouse gases. Efficiency is likely to be improved dramatically, and CO2 emissions reduced as a result, through combustion and gasification techniques which are now at the pilot and demonstration stages.\n\nClean coal is another way of improving the efficiency of fuel conversion. Research is under way into super-clean coal (3–5 per cent ash) and ultraclean coal (less than 1 per cent ash). Super-clean coal could increase the combustion efficiency of conventional pulverised fuel power plants. Ultraclean coal will allow coal to be used in advanced power systems such as coal-fired gas turbines, which, when operated in combined cycle, could achieve much greater efficiency.\n\nThose who defend mining point out that, environmentally, coal mining has two important points in its favour: it makes only temporary use of the land, and it produces no toxic chemical wastes. By planning projects carefully in advance, putting pollution control measures in place, monitoring the effects of mining and rehabilitating mined areas, the coal industry keeps its impact on the neighbouring community, the immediate environment and the long-term capability of the land to a minimum.\n\nDust levels are controlled by spraying roads and stockpiles, and water pollution is controlled by carefully separating clean runoff water from runoff which contains sediments or salt from the mine workings. The latter is treated and reused for dust suppression. Noise is controlled by modifying equipment and by putting insulation and sound enclosures around machinery.\n\nSince mining is only a temporary use of the land, extensive rehabilitation measures are taken to make sure that the land's capability after mining meets agreed and appropriate standards, which in some cases are better than its condition before mining. Where the mining is underground, the surface can be used at the same time for forests, cattle grazing and crop raising, or even reservoirs and urban development, with little or no disruption to the existing land use. In all cases, mining is subject to strict controls and approval processes."
